From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Robin=20Br=C3=A4mer?= <robin.braemer@web.de>
Date: Tue, 29 Sep 2026 13:42:14 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 5/5] vendor: standalone manifests for the three patched crates
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

kcl-parser, kcl-language-server, and kcl-runtime use Cargo workspace
inheritance (version.workspace = true, kcl-lexer.workspace = true,
etc.) that only resolves inside kcl-lang/kcl's own workspace root.
akuapkg vendors these three crates directly (via Cargo [patch] path
overrides) to carry the wasm32 fixes above without depending on a git
fork, so each manifest needs concrete values instead of workspace
inheritance, an explicit empty [workspace] table (so Cargo treats it
as a standalone package rather than trying - and failing - to fold it
into whatever workspace roots its parent directory), and its kcl-*
sibling dependencies declared the same way kcl-lang/lib (the wrapper
crate akuapkg actually depends on) declares them: plain
`git = "https://github.com/kcl-lang/kcl"` with no explicit rev.

That last point is load-bearing, not cosmetic: Cargo unifies a crate
across the dependency graph by source identity, and a `?rev=<sha>`
git source is a different identity than the same URL with no rev, even
when both resolve to the identical commit. Pinning only inside these
three manifests while kcl-lang/lib's own declaration stays unpinned
produced two incompatible copies of kcl-ast (and other shared types)
in the same build - "multiple different versions of crate kcl_ast in
the dependency graph" - so the pin lives solely in the committed
Cargo.lock, exactly like every other kcl-* crate in this workspace.

kcl-language-server additionally had two non-workspace path
dependencies (kcl-tools at "../../../tools", proc_macro_crate at
"../../benches/proc_macro_crate") rewritten the same way, for the same
reason.

Signed-off-by: Robin Brämer <robin.braemer@web.de>
